findings.body:
The concrete class was renamed from `SelectedCalendarRepository` to `PrismaSelectedCalendarRepository`, but no consumer was updated. The cron route `apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts` still imports `SelectedCalendarRepository` and calls `new SelectedCalendarRepository(prisma)` at line 41. The webhook route `apps/web/app/api/webhooks/calendar-subscription/[provider]/route.ts` does the same at line 61. The repository test imports the old name at line 3 and constructs it at line 53.

The export no longer exists under that name, so type-checking fails. At runtime, `new undefined(prisma)` throws a TypeError, so the subscription cron stops creating subscriptions and the webhook handler throws on every request.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="5-5"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The rename is not needed for this feature. Reverting it is the smallest fix. If the rename is intentional, every import and instantiation has to move in the same PR.

`getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` ignores the provider and flattens every entry in `GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES`. The Google suffixes are therefore applied to Office 365 subscriptions too, even though the map is keyed by provider. The per-provider structure is lost at the boundary, and the `office365_calendar: []` entry looks meaningful but does nothing.

The result is harmless today because Office 365 `externalId` values will not end in Google domains. Future changes to the map will be surprising, though. A provider-scoped API such as `getGenericCalendarSuffixes(provider)` would keep the mapping honest.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-71"}

`genericCalendarSuffixes` is optional in both the interface and the Prisma implementation. When a caller omits it, or passes an empty array, `AND` becomes `undefined` and the query returns generic calendars without any error or log.

The only production caller passes the list, so nothing is broken today. The failure mode is that a future caller, or a refactor that drops the argument, quietly reintroduces the bug this PR exists to fix. The safer contract is to make the parameter required so TypeScript catches omissions. Callers that really want no filtering can pass an empty array explicitly.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.interface.ts" lines="26-36"}

Two small cleanups. In the implementation, `genericCalendarSuffixes?.map` repeats the optional chain after the `?.length` check has already narrowed the value. A plain `genericCalendarSuffixes.map` is correct after the guard.

In the test file, the `AND: undefined` lines added to the three existing `findNextSubscriptionBatch` expectations at lines 134, 164, and 194 do nothing. Vitest `toHaveBeenCalledWith` treats undefined properties as absent, so these lines only add noise. They also make the test look like it checks the AND clause when it does not.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}

The new repository test asserts the exact Prisma `where` object, so it is tied to how the filter is built. It does not check that generic calendars are actually excluded, and nothing tests the empty-array path (`genericCalendarSuffixes: []`), which should produce no AND clause. The service test mocks `getGenericCalendarSuffixes` with a hard-coded Google list, so it never exercises the real factory output, and the factory itself has no unit test.

A focused factory test that asserts `getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` returns the four Google suffixes would catch an accidental edit to the constant. Adding an empty-array case to the repository test would cover the branch the human checklist asks about.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts" lines="202-232"}

The filter only changes which rows are selected for a new subscription. Generic calendars that are already subscribed keep their Google channel until it expires. Because the renewal path also goes through `findNextSubscriptionBatch`, those calendars are not renewed once they expire, and the webhook can keep delivering notifications for them until then.

This is probably the intended behavior, since the subscription simply lapses, but the PR description does not say so. Reviewers should confirm that no backfill is needed and that the lapse is acceptable.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/lib/CalendarSubscriptionService.ts" lines="391-397"}
